TARGET:
Derive a refutation-depth label alongside accept/refute/suspend without changing the skeptical semantics: a node is refuted at depth k when it is attacked by a node accepted at depth k-1, depths resolved by node-id tie-break so the computation remains a pure deterministic function of the graph and stays polynomial. The criticizable claim: depth is not invariant under isomorphic relabelling because the tie-break depends on node ids, and on cyclic attack chains the depth labels may be unstable or misleading, so the derived grading could be presented as meaningful evidence when it is an artifact of ordering.

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
SRC_002's depth label depends on a node-id tie-break, which the target itself flags as non-invariant under isomorphic relabelling. The problem demands proposals that preserve determinism of the fixed point and the validity of committed roots. Although the accept/refute/suspend status may remain deterministic, the derived refutation-depth grading is presented as meaningful evidence while being an artifact of arbitrary identifier ordering: two isomorphic graphs differing only in node-id assignment yield different depth labels for corresponding nodes. This is a substance defect: the grading is not a semantic invariant and can mislead, so the extension does not preserve the intended validity of the derived evidence despite preserving the core fixed point.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's case establishes only one fact: depth labels are not invariant under isomorphic relabelling. That is conceded by the target itself and is inherent to any node-id tie-break. However, this single fact does not establish a substance defect under the stated requirements. (1) The problem demands preservation of skeptical semantics and committed-root validity: both are preserved. Accept/refute/suspend is unchanged, the fixed point is deterministic, and committed roots remain valid. The critic concedes this. (2) The problem does not demand isomorphism-invariance of derived grading, so non-invariance is not by itself a violation. (3) The claim that the grading 'is presented as meaningful evidence' is not established: the target describes the label as a derived artifact of a polynomial deterministic function, not as a semantic invariant. That corresponding nodes in isomorphic graphs can receive different depth labels shows the label is ordering-relative, not that the extension is defective. (4) The claim of instability or misleadingness on cyclic attack chains is asserted but not demonstrated: the tie-break makes the function total and deterministic on every graph, cyclic or not, so instability is not shown. (5) Because the core semantics, fixed-point determinism, and committed-root validity are all preserved, the critic identifies a cosmetic non-invariance, not a substance defect. The case fails unless an independent requirement of isomorphism-invariance or semantic canonicity for the depth label is established, which the critic has not done.

